<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kharda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Kurdla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kharda was fought in March 1795 near Kharda in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> and the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, united for the last time under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> Phadnavis, won decisively, and the Nizam had to cede territory and pay a large indemnity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharda.jpg" alt="Battle of Kharda" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rushikesh Awatade · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kharda_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, also called the Battle of Khurla, was fought on 11 March 1795 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>. The Nizam was under British protection, but the Governor General, John Shore, kept to a policy of non-intervention and sent him no help, which strained relations between the Nizam and the British. It was the last occasion on which all the Maratha chiefs fought united.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Nizam's forces were commanded by Assud Ally, supported by the French commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-raymond">Michel Raymond</a>, and faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> troops led in the field by Parshuram Bhau, Scindia and Holkar. The Nizam is said to have fielded 17,000 infantry and cavalry against 83,000 Maratha cavalry, 38,000 infantry and 192 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. Early in the battle Lal Khan, a Pathan commander in the Nizam's army, wounded Parshuram Bhau but was later killed by Hari Pant.</p><p>Raymond's infantry attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, but Jivabadada Kerkar, leading Scindia's forces, countered and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> troops back to the fort of Kharda. The Marathas laid siege to the fort and cut off its supplies. The siege lasted 17 to 22 days and brought extreme hardship to the Nizam's men and animals: many ate tamarind leaves, and water was reportedly sold for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> a cup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under this pressure the Nizam sought terms, and the negotiations ended in April 1795. He agreed to dismiss his minister Azeem-ul-Omrah, who had insulted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>, and ceded territory including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solapur">Solapur</a>. The victory showed the military strength of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, while the Nizam's experience of British inaction strained his relationship with them and changed the political balance of the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kharda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeats by the Marathas at Udgir in 1760 and <b>Kharda</b> in 1795 cost the state large territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
